tml 2
// "Eighth Baffling Incongruity Theorem" (8-BIT) — eight-tone equal
// temperament, 8/8 time, an eight-beat groove, rendered by an 8-bit engine.
// One of Microtone's demo songs: the project is this program's output.
//
// THE THEOREM. 8-EDO (150-cent steps) is a zeta valley: no equal tuning with
// fewer steps plays the harmonic series worse. On an ordinary instrument its
// chords grind. But play it on instruments whose partials lie on its own grid
// and the two smoothest triads within the octave are 0-3-6 (450 + 450 cents)
// and 0-3-5 (450 + 300, call it major), with 0-2-5 (300 + 450, minor) close
// behind (all but tied with 0-5-7, a fifth with 300 on top). The three are
// ONE chord: the pitch classes {r, r+3, r+5} heard from each of its three
// notes. So 8-EDO's consonant triad comes on each of the eight notes, every
// one major, minor and its own mirror image at once — and stepping the root by
// 3 moves one voice by one step, so all eight form a single cycle that climbs
// for ever (the Proof). (Measured with the Timbre designer's model, Microtone's
// core/tuning/timbre.js; the order past the first two depends on the timbre.)
//
// Every pitched instrument is an FM rack of sine partials on the 8-EDO grid,
// designed by edotimbre.mjs and written below by make-timbres.mjs (the block
// between the TIMBRES markers) — tools kept outside Microtone's repository,
// over the same maths as the Timbre designer (Design timbre… on an FM rack). PLAIN is the control: the same recipe on the
// plain harmonic series, which is what 8-EDO sounds like without the theorem's
// hypothesis.
//
// Pitches are steps of 8-TET from C0: C D E F F# G# A B, 'C4' = 32, +8 an
// octave. A row is a sixteenth: two to the eighth, sixteen to the bar.
//
// Form (bars): 1–8 Statement · 9–16 Lemma 1 · 17–24 Lemma 2 · 25–32 Lemma 3 ·
// 33–48 Proof · 49–56 Counterexample · 57–72 Corollary · 73–80 Q.E.D.

song {
  title: "Eighth Baffling Incongruity Theorem"
  format: 3                   // volumes 0–255, the volume column's own units
  composer: "CuriousTorvald via Motif"
  copyright: "(c) 2026 CuriousTorvald"
  message: ABOUT
  tuning: "8-TET"
  tempo: 128
  speed: 6
  bars: 80
  bar_rows: 16
  beat_rows: 2                // a beat band every eighth: 8/8
  cue_bars: 4
  global_volume: 128
  mixing_volume: 100
  seed: 8
}

master {
  trim_db: 0
  hp_on: true, hp_freq: 28, hp_slope: 12
  eq_on: true
  eq: [
    { on: true, type: "low_shelf",  freq: 90,   gain_db: 1.5,  q: 0.707 },
    { on: true, type: "peak",       freq: 350,  gain_db: -1.5, q: 0.9 },
    { on: true, type: "peak",       freq: 3200, gain_db: 1,    q: 0.8 },
    { on: true, type: "high_shelf", freq: 8000, gain_db: 2,    q: 0.707 },
  ]
  comp_on: true, comp_detector: "rms", comp_thresh_db: -18, comp_ratio: 2
  comp_attack_ms: 20, comp_release_ms: 200, comp_knee_db: 6, comp_makeup_db: 3
  width_on: true, width: 1.2
  lim_on: true, lim_true_peak: false, lim_ceiling_db: -0.3, lim_release_ms: 30
  out_gain_db: 0
}

// ── mixing chains ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
chain ROOM   { name: "Room", mode: "bus", reverb: { size: 0.5, decay: 1.8, damping: 7000, mix: 0.18 } }
// The bell echoes three eighths later — the first group of 3 + 3 + 2.
chain ECHO   { name: "Echo 3+3+2", tape_delay: { time_ticks: 36, feedback: 0.3, high_cut: 6000, ping_pong: true, mix: 0.28 },
               reverb: { size: 0.6, decay: 2.2, mix: 0.18 } }
chain LEADFX { name: "Lead", tape_delay: { time_ticks: 18, feedback: 0.22, high_cut: 5000, mix: 0.16 },
               reverb: { size: 0.45, decay: 1.4, mix: 0.12 } }
chain HATS   { name: "Hats", equaliser_1: { bands: [{ shape: "high_pass", freq: 5000, slope: 24 }] } }

// ─── BEGIN TIMBRES ───
// Generated by make-timbres.mjs from edotimbre.mjs — do not edit by hand; change
// the SPECS there and run `make timbres`. The sine operators the racks read:
instrument W_KEYS { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [1.248, 130], [2.495, 65], [3.743, 32], [4.991, 16], [6.238, 8], [7.486, 4], [8.734, 0]] }
instrument W_D0050 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [1.386, 130], [2.773, 65], [4.159, 32], [5.545, 16], [6.931, 8], [8.318, 4], [9.704, 0]] }
instrument W_D0126 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.55, 130], [1.1, 65], [1.65, 32], [2.201, 16], [2.751, 8], [3.301, 4], [3.851, 0]] }
instrument W_D0159 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.437, 130], [0.873, 65], [1.31, 32], [1.747, 16], [2.183, 8], [2.62, 4], [3.057, 0]] }
instrument W_D0200 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.347, 130], [0.693, 65], [1.04, 32], [1.386, 16], [1.733, 8], [2.079, 4], [2.426, 0]] }
instrument W_KEYS_BITE { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.017, 130], [0.035, 65], [0.052, 32], [0.069, 16], [0.087, 8], [0.104, 4], [0.121, 0]] }
instrument W_PAD { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 0], [0.6, 255], [2, 0]], sustain: 1 }
instrument SINE_F24 { wave: "sine" }
instrument W_BASS { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.122, 227], [0.35, 162], [0.43, 0]], sustain: 2 }
instrument W_D0126H35 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.55, 130], [0.833, 89]] }
instrument W_D0252H35 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.275, 130], [0.417, 89]] }
instrument W_D0317H35 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.218, 130], [0.331, 89]] }
instrument W_BASS_BITE { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.028, 130], [0.055, 65], [0.083, 32], [0.111, 16], [0.139, 8], [0.166, 4], [0.194, 0]] }
instrument W_LEAD { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 0], [0.02, 255], [0.2, 0]], sustain: 1 }
instrument SINE { wave: "sine" }
instrument W_BELL { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [1.802, 130], [3.604, 65], [5.407, 32], [7.209, 16], [9.011, 8], [10.813, 4], [12.615, 0]] }
instrument W_D0050_F40 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [1.386, 130], [2.773, 65], [4.159, 32], [5.545, 16], [6.931, 8], [8.318, 4], [9.704, 0]] }
instrument W_D0063_F40 { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [1.1, 130], [2.201, 65], [3.301, 32], [4.401, 16], [5.502, 8], [6.602, 4], [7.702, 0]] }
instrument W_TWIN { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 0], [0.01, 255], [0.26, 0]], sustain: 1 }
instrument SINE_F40 { wave: "sine" }

// Grid Keys: struck keys for the chords, designed at C4.
instrument KEYS { name: "Grid Keys", nna: "cut", chain: ROOM, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_KEYS, ratio: 1, level: -4.25 },   // h1 → step 0 (C)
    { inst: W_D0050, ratio: 2 ** (8 / 8), level: -10.27 },   // h2 → step 8 (C +1 oct)
    { inst: W_D0126, ratio: 2 ** (13 / 8), level: -13.79 },   // h3 → step 13 (G# +1 oct)
    { inst: W_D0159, ratio: 2 ** (15 / 8), level: -16.29 },   // h4 → step 15 (B +1 oct)
    { inst: W_D0200, ratio: 2 ** (18 / 8), level: -18.23 },   // h5 → step 18 (E +2 oct)
    { inst: W_KEYS_BITE, ratio: 1, level: -9 },   // FM: bends the fundamental's phase at the attack
  ], algorithm: "0[5] + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4" } }

// Valley Pad: a held pad, designed at C3 where its voicings sit.
instrument PAD { name: "Valley Pad", nna: "off", fadeout: 24, chain: ROOM, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_PAD, ratio: 1, level: -1.99 },   // h1 → step 0 (C)
    { inst: SINE_F24, ratio: 2 ** (8 / 8), level: -11.02 },   // h2 → step 8 (C +1 oct)
    { inst: SINE_F24, ratio: 2 ** (13 / 8), level: -16.3 },   // h3 → step 13 (G# +1 oct)
    { inst: SINE_F24, ratio: 2 ** (16 / 8), level: -20.05 },   // h4 → step 16 (C +2 oct)
    { inst: SINE_F24, ratio: 2 ** (20 / 8), level: -22.96 },   // h5 → step 20 (F# +2 oct)
  ], algorithm: "0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4" } }

// Eight-Bit Bass: a plucked bass, designed at C2 — so low that only four partials, spread wide, stay smooth.
instrument BASS { name: "Eight-Bit Bass", nna: "cut", fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_BASS, ratio: 1, level: -3.45 },   // h1 → step 0 (C)
    { inst: W_D0126H35, ratio: 2 ** (8 / 8), level: -9.47 },   // h2 → step 8 (C +1 oct)
    { inst: W_D0252H35, ratio: 2 ** (13 / 8), level: -13 },   // h3 → step 13 (G# +1 oct)
    { inst: W_D0317H35, ratio: 2 ** (16 / 8), level: -15.49 },   // h4 → step 16 (C +2 oct)
    { inst: W_BASS_BITE, ratio: 1, level: -6 },   // FM: bends the fundamental's phase at the attack
  ], algorithm: "0[4] + 1 + 2 + 3" } }

// Theorem Lead: the lead, designed at C5.
instrument LEAD { name: "Theorem Lead", nna: "cut", chain: LEADFX, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_LEAD, ratio: 1, level: -4.86 },   // h1 → step 0 (C)
    { inst: SINE, ratio: 2 ** (8 / 8), level: -10.88 },   // h2 → step 8 (C +1 oct)
    { inst: SINE, ratio: 2 ** (14 / 8), level: -33.0 },   // h3 → step 14 (A +1 oct)
    { inst: SINE, ratio: 2 ** (16 / 8), level: -16.9 },   // h4 → step 16 (C +2 oct)
    { inst: SINE, ratio: 2 ** (19 / 8), level: -39.0 },   // h5 → step 19 (F +2 oct)
    { inst: SINE, ratio: 2 ** (20 / 8), level: -20.42 },   // h6 → step 20 (F# +2 oct)
  ], algorithm: "0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5" } }

// Lemma Bell: a bell, designed at C6, free to choose its partials among the first fourteen harmonics and to stray two steps.
instrument BELL { name: "Lemma Bell", nna: "off", fadeout: 40, chain: ECHO, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_BELL, ratio: 1, level: -2.04 },   // h1 → step 0 (C)
    { inst: W_D0050_F40, ratio: 2 ** (15 / 8), level: -10.34 },   // h3 → step 15 (B +1 oct)
    { inst: W_D0050_F40, ratio: 2 ** (17 / 8), level: -14.5 },   // h4 → step 17 (D +2 oct)
    { inst: W_D0063_F40, ratio: 2 ** (18 / 8), level: -18.65 },   // h5 → step 18 (E +2 oct)
  ], algorithm: "0 + 1 + 2 + 3" } }

// Tempered Twin: PLAIN's own recipe and envelope with its partials moved onto the grid, designed at C4: the other half of the experiment.
instrument TWIN { name: "Tempered Twin", nna: "off", fadeout: 40, chain: ROOM, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_TWIN, ratio: 1, level: -4.86 },   // h1 → step 0 (C)
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 2 ** (8 / 8), level: -10.88 },   // h2 → step 8 (C +1 oct)
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 2 ** (13 / 8), level: -14.4 },   // h3 → step 13 (G# +1 oct)
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 2 ** (16 / 8), level: -16.9 },   // h4 → step 16 (C +2 oct)
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 2 ** (19 / 8), level: -18.84 },   // h5 → step 19 (F +2 oct)
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 2 ** (21 / 8), level: -20.42 },   // h6 → step 21 (G# +2 oct)
  ], algorithm: "0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5" } }

// Plain Harmonic: the control: the first six harmonics, untuned to the grid.
instrument PLAIN { name: "Plain Harmonic", nna: "off", fadeout: 40, chain: ROOM, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_TWIN, ratio: 1, level: -4.86 },   // harmonic 1
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 2, level: -10.88 },   // harmonic 2
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 3, level: -14.4 },   // harmonic 3
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 4, level: -16.9 },   // harmonic 4
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 5, level: -18.84 },   // harmonic 5
    { inst: SINE_F40, ratio: 6, level: -20.42 },   // harmonic 6
  ], algorithm: "0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5" } }

// ── drums: FM racks of sines and noise (not tuned: they have no pitch to keep) ──
instrument W_KICK  { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.04, 186], [0.16, 73], [0.32, 16], [0.42, 0]] }
instrument W_THUMP { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.012, 81], [0.04, 0]] }
instrument W_NOISE_S { wave: "noise", length: 8192, envelope: [[0, 255], [0.03, 121], [0.09, 40], [0.2, 0]] }
instrument W_BODY  { wave: "sine", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.03, 105], [0.1, 24], [0.16, 0]] }
instrument W_NOISE_H { wave: "noise", length: 8192, envelope: [[0, 255], [0.018, 73], [0.05, 0]] }
instrument W_NOISE_O { wave: "noise", length: 8192, envelope: [[0, 255], [0.08, 121], [0.3, 24], [0.42, 0]] }
instrument W_METAL { wave: "square", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.03, 49], [0.06, 0]] }
instrument W_NOISE_C { wave: "noise", length: 8192, envelope: [[0, 255], [0.15, 138], [0.8, 49], [1.8, 12], [2.4, 0]] }
instrument W_RING  { wave: "square", envelope: [[0, 255], [0.4, 121], [1.6, 0]] }
// Kick: a sine whose phase a sine at the same pitch kicks for 40 ms. Played
// with a pitch slide down (E) from C3.
instrument KICK  { name: "FM Kick", percussion: true, nna: "cut", fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_KICK, level: 4 }, { inst: W_THUMP, level: -4 } ], algorithm: "0[1]" } }
// Snare: a short sine body and a noise, side by side.
instrument SNARE { name: "FM Snare", percussion: true, nna: "cut", fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_BODY, level: 1 }, { inst: W_NOISE_S, fixed: 'C1', level: 2 } ], algorithm: "0 + 1" } }
// Hats: noise ring-modulated by a square an octave and a 750-cent fifth apart.
instrument HAT   { name: "FM Hat", percussion: true, nna: "cut", chain: HATS, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_NOISE_H, fixed: 'C1' }, { inst: W_METAL, fixed: 'G#7', level: -1 } ], algorithm: "0 * 1 + 0" } }
// Crash: a long noise, half of it ring-modulated by a square a step below the hats'.
instrument CRASH { name: "FM Crash", percussion: true, nna: "off", fadeout: 30, chain: HATS, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_NOISE_C, fixed: 'C1', level: -3 }, { inst: W_RING, fixed: 'G#6', level: -2 } ], algorithm: "0 * 1 + 0" } }
instrument OHAT  { name: "FM Open Hat", percussion: true, nna: "cut", chain: HATS, fm: { operators: [
    { inst: W_NOISE_O, fixed: 'C1', level: -3 }, { inst: W_METAL, fixed: 'G#7', level: -1 } ], algorithm: "0 * 1 + 0" } }
// ─── END TIMBRES ───

// ── lanes: pan $00 left … $80 centre … $100 right ─────────────────────────────
lane kick  { inst: KICK,  pan: $80, label: "Kick" }
lane snare { inst: SNARE, pan: $76, label: "Snare", jitter: 8 }
lane hat   { inst: HAT,   pan: $A4, label: "Hats",  jitter: 12, trim: 0.7 }
lane cym   { inst: CRASH, pan: $5C, label: "Crash" }
lane bass  { inst: BASS,  pan: $80, label: "Bass" }
lane k1    { inst: KEYS,  pan: $5C, label: "Keys 1", trim: 0.85 }
lane k2    { inst: KEYS,  pan: $80, label: "Keys 2", trim: 0.85 }
lane k3    { inst: KEYS,  pan: $A4, label: "Keys 3", trim: 0.85 }
lane p1    { inst: PAD,   pan: $3C, label: "Pad 1", trim: 1.25 }
lane p2    { inst: PAD,   pan: $80, label: "Pad 2", trim: 1.25 }
lane p3    { inst: PAD,   pan: $C4, label: "Pad 3", trim: 1.25 }
lane lead  { inst: LEAD,  pan: $78, label: "Lead" }
lane alto  { inst: LEAD,  pan: $98, label: "Alto", trim: 0.8 }
lane bell  { inst: BELL,  pan: $B4, label: "Bell", trim: 0.55 }

let KEYS_L = [k1, k2, k3]
let PADS = [p1, p2, p3]

section "Statement"      1..8
section "Lemma 1"        9..16
section "Lemma 2"        17..24
section "Lemma 3"        25..32
section "Proof"          33..48
section "Counterexample" 49..56
section "Corollary"      57..72
section "Q.E.D."         73..80

// ── time: 8/8, grouped 3 + 3 + 2 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
let E = 2                                   // rows to the eighth
let GROUPS = [0, 3, 6]                      // where the bar's three groups start, in eighths
let GROUP_LEN = [3, 3, 2]

// ── harmony: the one triad ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// T(r) = {r, r+3, r+5}. Read from its bass: on r it is 450 + 300 ("major"),
// on r+5 it is 300 + 450 ("minor"), on r+3 it is 450 + 450 ("wide") — so the
// chord with bass b is major(b) = T(b), minor(b) = T(b+5), wide(b) = T(b+3). A
// chord here is its pitch classes (root + ivs, for nearest_chord) and its bass,
// with the bass's own fifth (750 c above it when the chord has one).
fn pc(x) => (x % 8 + 8) % 8
fn T(r) => { root: pc(r), ivs: [0, 3, 5] }
fn major(b) => merge(T(b), { bass: pc(b), fifth: 5, name: "major" })
fn minor(b) => merge(T(b + 5), { bass: pc(b), fifth: 5, name: "minor" })
fn wide(b) => merge(T(b + 3), { bass: pc(b), fifth: 3, name: "wide" })
fn pcs(ch) => [pc(ch.root + i) for i in ch.ivs]
fn tones_in(ch, lo, hi) => [p for p in lo..hi if contains(pcs(ch), pc(p))]
fn voicings(start, chords, lo, hi) => scan(chords, start, fn(v, c) => nearest_chord(v, c, lo, hi))
fn n(p) => note_name(p)

// Statement, the Lemmas and the Corollary: a bar each.
//   C minor · G# major (the SAME three notes) · E major · B major ·
//   G# minor (E major's notes again) · F minor · C major · E major
let PROG_A = [minor(0), major(5), major(2), major(7), minor(5), minor(3), major(0), major(2)]
// Lemma 3: the wide reading, and the minors.
let PROG_B = [wide(1), minor(3), wide(6), major(2), wide(4), minor(7), major(5), major(2)]
// The Proof: T(0), T(3), T(6), … — the root up 3 each time (down a 750-cent
// fifth), one voice moving one step, through all eight and home.
let CYCLE = [major(3 * k) for k in 0..<8]

// ── drums ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn kick_at(bar, row, vol, from = 'E2') => hit(kick, at(bar, row), from, vol, "E", $0300)
fn snare_at(bar, row, vol) => hit(snare, at(bar, row), 'E3', vol)
fn hat_at(bar, row, vol) => hit(hat, at(bar, row), 'C5', vol)
fn ohat_at(bar, row, vol) => hit(hat, at(bar, row), 'C5', vol, inst: OHAT)
fn crash(bar, vol, row = 0) => hit(cym, at(bar, row), 'C5', vol)
fn feel(what, bar, row) => 4 * random_int(-3, 3, what, bar, row)

// The eight beat: hats on every eighth, the kick on the first two groups, the
// snare on 2 and 4 — 4 being where the last group of 3 + 3 + 2 begins.
fn groove(bar, level = 0, fill_from = 8, hats = true, open = true) {
  let ok = fn(e) => e < fill_from
  for [e, v] in [[0, 227], [3, 202]] if ok(e) { kick_at(bar, e * E, v + level) }
  if ok(5) and chance(0.35, "kick and", bar) { kick_at(bar, 5 * E, 162 + level) }
  if ok(7) and chance(0.3, "kick pickup", bar) { kick_at(bar, 7 * E + 1, 138 + level) }
  for [e, v] in [[2, 202], [6, 219]] if ok(e) { snare_at(bar, e * E, v + level) }
  if ok(4) and chance(0.4, "ghost", bar) { snare_at(bar, 4 * E + 1, 57 + level) }
  if hats {
    let open_at = if open then choose([none, none, 7, 5], "open hat", bar) else none
    for e in 0..<8 if ok(e) {
      if e == open_at { ohat_at(bar, e * E, 154 + level) }
      else { hat_at(bar, e * E, (if contains(GROUPS, e) then 186 else 130) + level + feel("hat", bar, e)) }
      if e != open_at and chance(0.18, "hat sixteenth", bar, e) { hat_at(bar, e * E + 1, 65 + level) }
    }
  }
}
// A half-time groove for the Counterexample: kick on the bar, snare on 4.
fn half_groove(bar, level = 0) {
  kick_at(bar, 0, 202 + level)
  snare_at(bar, 6 * E, 162 + level)
  for e in [0, 3, 6] { hat_at(bar, e * E, 121 + level + feel("half hat", bar, e)) }
}
// Fills from the eighth `from` to the bar's end: a snare roll, or toms (the
// kick struck higher, sliding down), chosen by the bar.
fn roll(bar, from) => [snare_at(bar, r, 73 + (r - from * E) * 12) for r in from * E..<16]
fn toms(bar, from) {
  let pitches = ['C4', 'A3', 'F#3', 'E3', 'D3', 'C3', 'A2', 'G#2']
  for i, r in [r for r in from * E..<16 if r % 2 == 0 or r >= 12] {
    hit(kick, at(bar, r), pitches[min(i, 7)], 162 + i * 8, "E", $0180)
  }
}
fn fill(bar, from) => if chance(0.5, "fill", bar) then roll(bar, from) else toms(bar, from)

// ── bass: 3 + 3 + 2 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Its pitch for a pitch class, between F#1 and F2.
fn bpitch(c) => 'F#1' + pc(c - 4)
fn bass_bar(bar, ch, vol = 202, style = 0) {
  let b = bpitch(ch.bass)
  let f = b + ch.fifth
  let second = if style == 1 then b + 8 else b
  melody(bass, at(bar), "${n(b)}:6@${vol + 8} ${n(second)}:6@${vol - 24} ${n(f)}:4@${vol - 8}")
}
// One long note a bar, for the quiet sections.
fn bass_held(bar, ch, vol = 186, bars = 1) => melody(bass, at(bar), "${n(bpitch(ch.bass))}:${16 * bars}@${vol}")

// ── keys: stabs on the groups; pads: held, gliding ───────────────────────────
let KEYS_START = notes("G#3 C4 E4")
fn keys_bars(bar, chords, vol = 170, inst = none, start = KEYS_START) {
  let vs = voicings(start, chords, 'F#3', 'A4')
  for i, v in vs {
    for g, e in GROUPS {
      for j, p in v { hit(KEYS_L[j], at(bar + i, e * E), p, vol - (if g == 0 then 0 else 20) - (if j == 0 then 8 else 0), inst: inst) }
    }
  }
}
// Held chords, a bar each (`inst` to play them on the twins).
fn held_bars(bar, chords, vol, inst, start = KEYS_START) {
  let vs = voicings(start, chords, 'F#3', 'A4')
  for i, v in vs { for j, p in v { hit(KEYS_L[j], at(bar + i), p, vol, inst: inst) } }
}
fn release_keys(row) => [keyoff(l, row) for l in KEYS_L]

let PAD_START = notes("C3 E3 G#3")
fn hold_chord(ids, row, steps, vol) => [hit(ids[i], row, s, vol) for i, s in steps]
fn glide_chord(ids, row, from, to, rows) =>
  [glide(ids[i], row, s, glide_speed(from[i], s, rows)) for i, s in to if s != from[i]]
// Strike the first chord of `events` ({ row, ch }), then glide into every other:
// a pad never strikes twice, it only moves the voice that has to move.
fn morph(ids, events, start, lo, hi, vol, rows = 2) {
  let vs = voicings(start, [e.ch for e in events], lo, hi)
  hold_chord(ids, events[0].row, vs[0], vol)
  for i in 1..<len(events) { glide_chord(ids, events[i].row, vs[i - 1], vs[i], rows) }
}
fn bar_events(chords, first_bar, every = 16) => [{ row: at(first_bar) + i * every, ch: c } for i, c in chords]

// ── the bell: eighths over the chord, 3 + 3 + 2 ─────────────────────────────
let BELL_FIG = [[0, 1, 2, 1, 2, 3, 2, 1], [2, 1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 3], [0, 2, 1, 3, 2, 4, 3, 1]]
fn bell_bar(bar, ch, vol = 121, from = 0, to = 8) {
  let fig = choose(BELL_FIG, "bell", bar)
  let ts = tones_in(ch, 'E5', 'E6')
  for e in from..<to { hit(bell, at(bar, e * E), ts[fig[e] % len(ts)], vol + (if contains(GROUPS, e) then 16 else 0)) }
}

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 1–8  STATEMENT — four chords on the PLAIN harmonic series, then the same four
// on its tempered twin: one recipe, one envelope, one voicing; only the
// partials have moved onto the grid
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
held_bars(1, slice(PROG_A, 0, 4), 162, PLAIN)
held_bars(5, slice(PROG_A, 0, 4), 162, TWIN)
release_keys(at(9))
for b in 1..8 { bass_held(b, PROG_A[(b - 1) % 4], 146) }
// a clock in the hats from bar 3, the groove's kick from bar 7
for b in 3..8 { for e in 0..<8 { hat_at(b, e * E, (if contains(GROUPS, e) then 138 else 89) + (b - 3) * 8) } }
kick_at(7, 0, 194)
kick_at(7, 3 * E, 170)
kick_at(8, 0, 202)
fill(8, 4)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 9–16  LEMMA 1 — the groove, the bass, the keys and the bell on PROG_A
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
crash(9, 186)
keys_bars(9, PROG_A + [], 162)
for i in 0..<8 {
  let b = 9 + i
  groove(b, fill_from: if b == 16 then 6 else 8)
  bass_bar(b, PROG_A[i], 194, style: i % 2)
  bell_bar(b, PROG_A[i], 105)
}
fill(16, 6)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 17–24  LEMMA 2 — the theme, written out note by note
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
let THEME = """
  E5:6 G#5:6 F#5:2 E5:2 | C6:6 G#5:6 E5:4 | B5:6 G#5:4 F#5:2 E5:4 | F#5:12v E5:4 |
  G#5:6 B5:6 C6:2 B5:2 | C6:6 A5:2 G#5:4 F5:4 | G#5:6 F5:6 E5:4 | E5:6 G#5:4 B5:6
  """
let THEME_TUNE = melody(lead, at(17), THEME, vol: 194, vib: [$60, $10])
THEME_TUNE
crash(17, 178)
keys_bars(17, PROG_A, 154)
morph(PADS, bar_events(PROG_A, 17), PAD_START, 'G#2', 'C4', 89)
for p in PADS { keyoff(p, at(25)) }
for i in 0..<8 {
  let b = 17 + i
  groove(b, fill_from: if b == 24 then 6 else 8)
  bass_bar(b, PROG_A[i], 202, style: i % 2)
  if i >= 4 { bell_bar(b, PROG_A[i], 89) }
}
fill(24, 6)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 25–32  LEMMA 3 — the wide reading: the bell sings a line chord_line() makes
// from PROG_B, three notes a bar on the groups
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
let B_LINE = chord_line(PROG_B, 'E5', 'E6', "lemma 3", per: 3, variety: 2, start: 'F#5', end: 'B5')
melody(bell, at(25), join(["${n(p)}:${GROUP_LEN[i % 3] * E}@${if i % 3 == 0 then 162 else 138}" for i, p in B_LINE], " "), ring: true)
// the alto answers it a group later, an octave down
on(alto, shift(transpose(melody(bell, at(25), join(["${n(p)}:${GROUP_LEN[i % 3] * E}" for i, p in B_LINE], " "), vol: 121), -8), 3 * E))
crash(25, 162)
morph(PADS, bar_events(PROG_B, 25), notes("D3 F#3 B3"), 'G#2', 'C4', 121)
for p in PADS { keyoff(p, at(33)) }
for i in 0..<8 {
  let b = 25 + i
  groove(b, level: -16, open: false, fill_from: if b == 32 then 4 else 8)
  bass_bar(b, PROG_B[i], 186)
}
keys_bars(25, PROG_B, 121, start: notes("B3 D4 F#4"))
fill(32, 4)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 33–48  PROOF — by induction over the eight triads. Each two bars the root
// goes up 3 (down a 750-cent fifth: C F A D F# B E G#) and one voice of the pad
// moves up one step; the lead's figure ends on the note the next figure begins
// on. Twice round would rise an octave: the pad climbs for ever.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
fn fold(r) => if pc(r) > 4 then pc(r) - 8 else pc(r)
fn figure(base) =>
  "${n(base + 5)}:6 ${n(base + 3)}:4 ${n(base + 5)}:2 ${n(base)}:4 | ${n(base + 3)}:6 ${n(base + 5)}:6 ${n(base + 8)}:4"
let PROOF_TUNE = seq([melody(lead, 0, figure('C5' + fold(3 * k)), vol: 186, vib: [$60, $10]) for k in 0..<8])
shift(PROOF_TUNE, at(33))
// …and from bar 41 the alto takes the same figures a group behind, an octave down: a canon at 3 eighths.
on(alto, shift(transpose(slice(PROOF_TUNE, at(9) - at(1), at(17) - at(1)), -8), at(41) - at(9) + 3 * E))
crash(33, 202)
crash(41, 186)
morph(PADS, bar_events(CYCLE + [major(0)], 33, 32), PAD_START, 'G#2', 'G#4', 130, rows: 4)
for p in PADS { keyoff(p, at(49)) }
for k in 0..<8 {
  for h in 0..1 {
    let b = 33 + 2 * k + h
    groove(b, level: if k < 4 then -8 else 8, fill_from: if b == 48 then 4 else 8)
    bass_bar(b, CYCLE[k], 202, style: h)
    if k >= 4 { bell_bar(b, CYCLE[k], 89) }
  }
}
keys_bars(33, flatten([[c, c] for c in CYCLE]), 138)
fill(48, 4)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 49–56  COUNTEREXAMPLE — drop the hypothesis: PROG_A's chords bar by bar, on
// PLAIN and its twin in turn
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
crash(49, 162)
for i in 0..<8 {
  let b = 49 + i
  held_bars(b, [PROG_A[i]], 154, if i % 2 == 0 then PLAIN else TWIN, start: notes("G#3 C4 E4"))
  bass_held(b, PROG_A[i], 162)
  half_groove(b, level: -16)
}
release_keys(at(57))
// the lead remembers the theme's first notes, slowly
melody(lead, at(51), "E5:12 G#5:12 F#5:8 | E5:24v r:8", vol: 138, vib: [$50, $10])
fill(56, 5)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 57–72  COROLLARY — the theme twice, the second time with the alto a chord
// tone below every note of it
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
shift(THEME_TUNE, at(57) - at(17))
shift(THEME_TUNE, at(65) - at(17))
fn below_in(ch, p) {
  let ts = tones_in(ch, p - 8, p - 1)
  return if len(ts) == 0 then p - 3 else ts[-1]
}
follow(lead, at(65), at(73), fn(heard) {
  for i, c in heard if c != none and has_key(c, "note") {
    let row = at(65) + i
    if c.note == "off" { keyoff(alto, row) }
    else if type(c.note) == "number" { hit(alto, row, below_in(PROG_A[floor(i / 16)], c.note), round(get(c, "vol", 194) * 0.75)) }
  }
})
crash(57, 210)
crash(65, 194)
keys_bars(57, PROG_A + PROG_A, 162)
morph(PADS, bar_events(PROG_A + PROG_A, 57), PAD_START, 'G#2', 'C4', 113)
for p in PADS { keyoff(p, at(73)) }
for i in 0..<16 {
  let b = 57 + i
  groove(b, level: 8, fill_from: if b == 64 then 6 else if b == 72 then 4 else 8)
  bass_bar(b, PROG_A[i % 8], 210, style: i % 2)
  bell_bar(b, PROG_A[i % 8], if i < 8 then 81 else 105)
}
fill(64, 6)
fill(72, 4)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 73–80  Q.E.D. — the cycle once more at four times the pace, then C minor,
// struck by everyone; then its bass moves to G# and the same three notes are
// G# major. ∎
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
crash(73, 202)
morph(PADS, bar_events(CYCLE + [minor(0)], 73, 8), PAD_START, 'G#2', 'G#4', 138, rows: 2)
keys_bars(73, [CYCLE[2 * i] for i in 0..<4], 146)    // a chord a bar while the pad turns twice as fast
for k in 0..<8 { bell_bar(73 + floor(k / 2), CYCLE[k], 105, from: 4 * (k % 2), to: 4 * (k % 2) + 4) }
for b in 73..76 {
  groove(b, level: 16, fill_from: if b == 76 then 4 else 8)
  bass_bar(b, CYCLE[2 * (b - 73)], 210)
}
melody(lead, at(73), join(["${n('C5' + fold(3 * k) + 5)}:8" for k in 0..<8], " ") + " | C5:32v>", vol: 178, vib: [$60, $10])
fill(76, 4)
// 77–78: C minor (C E G#), everyone
crash(77, 235)
kick_at(77, 0, 243)
hold_chord(KEYS_L, at(77), notes("G#3 C4 E4"), 178)
melody(bass, at(77), "C2:32@219")
melody(alto, at(77), "G#4:32v", vol: 146)
hit(bell, at(77), 'C6', 162)
// 79: the bass moves to G#: the same three notes, now G# major
melody(bass, at(79), "G#1:16@219")
hit(bell, at(79), 'G#5', 138)
hit(bell, at(79, 6), 'C6', 121)
hit(bell, at(79, 12), 'E6', 113)
// 80: C
melody(bass, at(80), "C2:16@202")
hit(kick, at(80), 'C3', 178, "E", $0300)
hit(bell, at(80), 'C6', 146)
crash(80, 146)
for l in PADS + KEYS_L + [lead, alto] {
  vol_ramp(l, at(78, 8), at(80, 14), 154, 32)
  keyoff(l, at(80, 15))
}
keyoff(bass, at(80, 15))

// ── the Project message ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
let ABOUT = """
  Eighth Baffling Incongruity Theorem — 8-BIT for short — is in eight-tone equal temperament and 8/8 time, over an eight-beat groove, played by an engine that renders in 8 bits. It is generated by a program written in Microtone's own music language; the last paragraph of this note says where to find it.

  A step of 8-EDO is 150 cents, three quarters of a whole tone. By the Riemann zeta function, the measure xenharmonic theory uses for how well an equal tuning plays the harmonic series, 8 is a zeta valley: no equal tuning with fewer steps does worse. Its nearest fifth is 750 cents, 48 out; its thirds are 300 and 450. Played on an ordinary instrument, whose overtones are the harmonic series, its chords grind.

  The theorem's hypothesis is the instrument: we forged our instruments to be consonant over the 8-EDO grid. Every pitched sound in this song is built from sine partials, and each partial sits on a step of 8-EDO instead of where the harmonic series would put it. Two such tones an 8-EDO interval apart have overtones that meet instead of beating, so the intervals that grind on an ordinary instrument hold still.

  The theorem: on such instruments the two smoothest triads are 450 + 450 cents and 450 + 300 (call it major), with 300 + 450 (minor) close behind — and the three are one chord, the same three notes heard from each of them in turn. So 8-EDO's consonant triad comes on each of its eight notes, and each is major, minor and its own mirror image at once. C minor (C E G#) is G# major; E major is G# minor. Stepping the root up by three moves just one voice of the chord, one step: the eight triads make a single cycle that climbs for ever.

  Bars 1–8, Statement: four chords on a plain harmonic instrument, then the same four on its twin, which differs only in that its overtones are on the grid. By Sethares' roughness model (after Plomp and Levelt) the twin plays them a third to two fifths smoother — its C minor about as smooth as a twelve-tone C major triad on the plain instrument.

  Bars 9–24, Lemmas 1 and 2: the groove, in eighths grouped 3 + 3 + 2, and the theme.

  Bars 25–32, Lemma 3: the wide reading of the chord, and a bell line the program writes from the harmony, answered by the alto a group later.

  Bars 33–48, Proof: by induction over the eight triads. Every two bars the root falls a fifth, one voice of the pad rises a step, and the lead's figure ends on the note the next figure begins on. In bar 41 the alto takes up the figures as a canon, three eighths behind.

  Bars 49–56, Counterexample: the hypothesis dropped. The harmony bar by bar on the plain instrument and on its twin in turn.

  Bars 57–72, Corollary: the theme twice, the second time with the alto a chord tone below it.

  Bars 73–80, Q.E.D.: the cycle at four times the pace, then C minor struck by everyone; then the bass moves to G#, and without a note changing above it, the chord is G# major.

  The Timeline is set to 8-TET notation: C D E F F# G# A B, eight notes to the octave. The beat bands mark eighths, a bar every sixteen rows.

  Nothing in this project was played or placed by hand: the notes, the instruments, the mix and the master are computed by a program in Taud Motif Language, its instruments' partials placed by the same search the Timbre designer makes (Design timbre… on an FM instrument), and this project is the result. The program travels inside the project: open the Motif view to read it, change it and build the song again.
  """
